workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-059-wp-t4-agreement.md:23:- **Owner decisions:** none in the register. The rulings this task rests on are in the ledger: OR-4 ("Yes, the most accommodating value wins"), OR-6 (stated and divergent, with a synthesis approach), OR-6b ("No, the spread is printed in the note") and OR-6c (tenth set, 2026-11-13). **Step 3 of the report (the Co-1 boundary) is not in this task**: OR-5b's test of soundness has three open edges (ledger, tenth set, "What it does not decide"), and no code is written on a guess (section 9). It becomes its own task when the owner has answered them.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-059-wp-t4-agreement.md:44:The owner's rulings fix the behaviour: the most accommodating value wins whatever anchoring tier stated it (OR-4); anchoring claims that cannot all hold are recorded `divergent`, stay `stated`, and are not written without a synthesis approach (OR-6; evidence-methodology §3.3); floors that differ but can all be met are not divergent and the spread is printed in the note (OR-6b); and for claims that cannot all hold, the approach for a T1 figure against a T2 figure is the owner's own: the T1 figure is stated and the T2 dispute is noted in the item's discussion. The tenth set (2026-11-13) extends that to **any divergence between different tiers**, with **Co-1 and T1 counted as one rank**: so a divergence between ranks is resolved by the higher rank stating the value and the lower rank's dispute being noted, and a divergence between two sources of the same rank (Co-1 against T1 included) waits for a synthesis approach written by an Opus-class session.
