workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:5:**Pilot status.** Not piloted. Authored 2026-11-13 (Opus-class, cluster B). **No longer held on Q-T068 (OD-43): resolved under delegation and confirmed by its independent check (2026-11-13), then answered by the owner (2026-11-14, thirteenth set): agreement between any two kinds of evidence counts as convergence, and convergence defines most common practice, which is not best practice. Section 2 reads it through the ledger's gate; step 3 of section 4 builds it. Refined the same day by the owner's statements (`grep -n -F "Owner statement 2026-11-14 — convergence means values coming together" references/project-standards.md`): convergence means values coming together, whatever the sources and across any tiers; its significance is read from the tiers of the converging sources; it is most common practice, never evidence of best practice. The carve-out that kept agreement among codes alone `pending_assessment` is withdrawn, and the root count is shown beside every convergence rather than used as a condition of it (title changed 2026-11-14 from "A cell is convergent only on two or more independent roots"). The same entry adds: two or more clusters on different values are divergence between sets of converged values and a cue to investigate (section 4, items 5 and 6, and "What the investigation looks at"); a working tolerance of 5% for "the same value"; and ranges as the general case, every figure grouped as the interval it states.** Its evidence commands were run on 2026-11-13 and printed what section 3 states; the shared view of item 1 was built on a scratch copy and reproduced `v_value_independence` exactly. Expect to find a defect in this file, and report it rather than work around it.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:23:- **Owner decisions:** OD-43 (this file's Q-T068: when may the engine call a cell `convergent`?), **answered by the owner 2026-11-14** (ledger: `grep -n -F "Owner answers 2026-11-14 (thirteenth set)" references/project-standards.md`): "yes convergence is possible too, and it helps define most common practice (which isn't same as best practice)", recorded as `OD-43: agreement between any two kinds of evidence counts as convergence; convergence defines most common practice, which is not best practice`. The recorder's readings, each marked [ASSUMPTION] in that entry and unratified: agreement between any two kinds of evidence counts (two tiers, or two sources of one tier: the owner was shown "two research studies, or a research study and a professional guideline" as cases that could disagree but never agree), symmetric with the owner's OR-6 and OR-6c use of `divergent`; the other conditions stand (the same value or the same range on the parameter's scale; anchoring claims that can all hold, OR-6b; two or more independent roots among the governing figures, sources sharing a root counting as one, H1); and a convergence output is most common practice, never labelled or rendered as best practice. The delegated resolution of 2026-11-13 (`grep -n -F "Delegated resolution 2026-11-13 — OD-43" references/project-standards.md`), which limited `convergent` to the clinical and Co-1 axes, is superseded on contact as to that limit and stays as history. **Refined the same day by nine owner statements** (ledger: `grep -n -F "Owner statement 2026-11-14 — convergence means values coming together" references/project-standards.md`; quote them from there): convergence means values coming together, among any sources and across any tiers (a Tier 1 and a Tier 6 source giving one value converge), codes alone included; its significance is read from the tiers and weight bands of the converging sources, never computed as a score; it is not evidence of best practice but most common practice; two or more clusters on different values are divergence between sets of converged values, which cues investigation; the investigation looks at the owner's starting list of causes; "the same value" is within a working tolerance of 5%; and ranges are included, as the general case. The recorder's reading of the thirteenth set that "two or more independent roots among the governing figures" is a condition of `convergent` is withdrawn: the root count is shown beside every convergence, not required for it. Every reading there is an [ASSUMPTION], unratified. Other rulings it rests on: the definition (`grep -n -F 'agree on the parameter value or range' governance/evidence-methodology.md`, now narrower than the owner's answer: a caller, section 5), OR-6b (`grep -n -F 'OR-6b: "No, the spread is printed in the note"' references/project-standards.md`), H1, and best practice as the most accommodating value (`grep -n -F 'best practice =/= most common or agreed upon' references/project-standards.md`; `grep -n -F 'is NOT evidence of best practice but most common practice' references/project-standards.md`). Run the gate before you start and again before you open the pull request; it must print `OD-43 OPEN (owner)`, else stop (section 9):
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:46:Twenty-four codes repeating one figure are one fact about transmission, not twenty-four facts about disabled people (`governance/evidence-architecture.md` §4.5). A cell called `convergent` is rendered as "Tier 1 OT evidence and Co-1 sources converge on [claim]" (`grep -n -F '| `convergent` | Co-1-C |' governance/evidence-methodology.md`), a sentence that names Tier 1 and Co-1 only; under the owner's answer to OD-43 (2026-11-14) it is a caller (section 5), and what converges is most common practice, never best practice. If two sources that rest on one measurement are counted as two, that sentence launders one root into corroboration (under the owner's statements of 2026-11-14 the two may still converge, but the statement must show one root). The root registry and `v_value_independence` exist to stop this, but nothing ties a `convergent` status to the root count: the engine does not write `convergent` today, the validator checks only axes (`grep -n -F "status 'convergent' requires ≥2 evidence axes" scripts/validate_evidence_state.py`), and the prior workplan's T-8 condition is in no task (T-048 builds the registry's writer only: `grep -c -i 'distinct' workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-048-root-registry-writer.md` prints 0).
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:97:   **Every convergence statement carries, beside the word** (in the rationale the engine already writes, and in the report's `convergence` record; no column, no score): the number of sources; the number of independent roots, counted through item 1's view (`SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT root) FROM v_value_independence_rows WHERE extraction_id IN (<the governing extraction ids>)`, the ids being the links `gather_extraction_links` marks `governing`; sources sharing a root are one root, and figures whose root is `committee_assertion` or `untraced` count none, so a code-only convergence reads one root where the codes share a measured root and **0** where their figure traces to none, shown, never hidden); and each converging source with its tier and its weight band, derived per source by `schemas/tier_derivation.py` `band(tier, evidence_type)` from the sources already linked (the significance of a convergence is read from those tiers: Tier 1 agreement weighs more than Tier 4 agreement; it is never computed as a figure). The rationale **states that the agreeing value or range is the most common practice among the governing figures; it never calls it best practice, and never says the convergence raises confidence.** A convergence among codes alone also carries the code-derived flag (`regulatory_stratum_only`) and the convergence-not-evidence caveat the ledger already requires for regulatory-only bases. **Convergence never changes which value the determination names as best practice** (the most accommodating value found in the parameter's recorded direction) **nor its marker** (●, ◐, ○): it is a status beside the value, not a way of choosing it (item 7). A rationale that cannot be true of the cell is never written; never "queued" for a cell whose evidence cannot meet the definition (G8's disclosure, `governance/evidence-methodology.md` §3.4, reads this rationale). **Withdrawn (2026-11-14):** the fold's "conservative" choice that kept agreement among regulatory sources alone `pending_assessment` with the rationale "agreement among regulatory sources only; not decided": the owner's statements decide it (codes alone converge). The proxy branch (findings supplying direction) is untouched: it never writes `convergent`. The engine writes no synthesis prose (the report's K-5).
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:109:   - **Never by size.** Divergence between clusters is never resolved by choosing the larger cluster: frequency is never evidence, and best practice stays the most accommodating value in the parameter's recorded direction (OR-4; `skills/run-pipeline_SKILL.md` §6 test 1).
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:110:7. **Convergence feeds no value, marker or state upward.** Owner, 2026-11-14: "convergence is not evidence of best practice but most common practice". A convergent assessment changes neither which value is named best practice nor the marker. **No code path lets it today** (read 2026-11-14; re-read before building): the marker is the floor mean of the governing sources' bands and never reads the convergence (`grep -n -A14 '^    marker = None' scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`); the state is set from the tiers present before the convergence record is built; the value comes from `compose_value` over the governing claims and the direction; the views that read `convergence_assessment` (`v_divergence`, `v_convergence_sources`) feed no state, marker or value; the validator reads the status only to refuse a `stated` cell resting on Tier 3 alone (`grep -n -F "single-axis convergence is" scripts/validate_evidence_state.py`), which lowers and never raises. Test T068-11 guards it.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:134:**What wrong thing reaches the guidebook without it:** a page that says sources "converge" on a figure without showing that it rests on one measurement repeated; a page that presents the value the evidence agrees on, which is most common practice, as best practice; a page that gives no sign that a convergence is among codes alone, or among Tier 1 studies; or a parameter whose evidence splits into two camps rendered as one headline with no sign that the field disagrees.
workplan/2026-11-12-bidirectional-pipeline/tasks/T-068-convergent-counts-roots.md:155:**Hits when this file was written** (2026-11-13): `v_value_independence`: `scripts/assess/assess_cell.py` (a comment), `scripts/db.py` (two comments), `scripts/tests/test_scan_contract.py` (Q9 reads it: run before and after), `skills/run-pipeline_SKILL.md` (a mention), `governance/evidence-architecture.md` §4.5 and `governance/pipeline-contract.yaml` `convergence-independence` (mentions: both already say roots are counted). `convergent`: `scripts/assess/assess_cell.py` (the comments at both `pending_assessment` assignments say the engine never claims `convergent`: callers, edited to say when it does), `scripts/validate_evidence_state.py` (item 2). Re-derive `pending_assessment` and `RULE_VERSION`. **Callers added by the owner's answer to OD-43 (2026-11-14):** `governance/evidence-methodology.md`'s definition of `convergent` ("Tier 1–3 clinical evidence and Co-1 evidence agree…") and its Co-1-C rendered sentence ("Tier 1 OT evidence ([sources]) and Co-1 sources ([sources]) converge on [claim]") both name only Tier 1 and Co-1 and say nothing of most common practice. **Widened by the owner's statements of 2026-11-14** (ledger: `grep -n -F "Owner statement 2026-11-14 — convergence means values coming together" references/project-standards.md`): in `governance/evidence-methodology.md` §3.2 and §3.4, (1) the `convergent` definition, both its axis limit and "The converged claim carries higher confidence than either dimension alone" (`grep -n -F 'carries higher confidence than either dimension alone' governance/evidence-methodology.md`); (2) the `single_axis` definition, whose "or Tier 4–6 only). No convergence or divergence assessment is possible" no longer stands, and which now means one source; (3) the `divergent` definition, which names clinical and Co-1 only and says nothing of clusters; (4) the voice-pattern Co-1-C sentence ("Tier 1 OT evidence ([sources]) and Co-1 sources ([sources]) converge on [claim]"), which must name the converging sources with their tiers and say most common practice; (5) the voice-pattern row for `single_axis` (Tier 4–6 only) (`grep -n -F "jurisdictions' standards converge on [value] — best practice as currently known" governance/evidence-methodology.md`), which joins a code convergence to "best practice" in one sentence and moves to `convergent`; and (6) §3.2's opening question ("the converged value carries higher confidence than either alone"). A reader only, to check and leave true: §2.3's richness criteria (`grep -n -F 'converging on the same value or range, establishing code consensus' governance/evidence-methodology.md`), where code convergence decides `provisional` against `pending` (a state of sufficiency, not a marker or value; the engine checks jurisdictions only: `grep -n -F 'value-level convergence unverified' scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`); and `governance/evidence-architecture.md` §4.5 ("precedent counts documents; evidence counts roots"), which stays true and is the reason the root count is shown. Code and test callers added: `scripts/assess/assess_cell.py`'s regulatory-only branch (hard-coded `single_axis`, item 3); `scripts/validate_evidence_state.py`'s axes rule and its docstring line "convergent needs ≥2 evidence axes, single_axis ≤1" (item 2); `scripts/tests/test_validate_evidence_state_2_4.py`'s docstring "convergent⇒≥2 axes"; `scripts/tests/test_assess_cell_pilot.py`'s PROXY tests (unchanged: the proxy branch never writes `convergent`); `schemas/evidence_state.py` (rationale rules; check they still hold); `.claude/commands/batch-done.md` (item 6). Mentions only, not callers: `v_convergence_sources`, `scripts/dbcore.py` `determinations_resting_on`, and the `conflicts` table (`add-conflict`), which records cross-population conflicts, not clusters, and is not used for the cue. Correct the doctrine sentences in this pull request to the owner's answer, keeping each old sentence in place with a marker line beside it that names SUPERSEDED and quotes the ledger entry it rests on (the thirteenth set, or the entry of the nine statements), append the `SUPERSEDES:` / `BY:` pair to the ledger in the form `scripts/audit/supersession_backpointer_audit.py` reads (its docstring), and run that audit before and after. Also sweep `most common`, `best practice` and `converge` in `governance/` and `skills/` for a sentence that equates agreement with best practice.
